#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460ddf19-9666-5bc1-9df3-8ca6fb7f1c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939} {:answer/id #uuid "47062968-266d-59c3-8ee9-6d389c557d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299940} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe8a53c-3546-5459-b3fd-157eb63fe59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299941} {:answer/id #uuid "9f88ab36-3369-5189-829e-d3990c850fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299942} {:answer/id #uuid "0692209c-b798-54c0-acfa-b887bfc91a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "460ddf19-9666-5bc1-9df3-8ca6fb7f1c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299939} :answer-field/id #uuid "061cc3fd-2b72-55b5-9c00-9a87ce785368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0eeec9a6-3e7c-537b-8fa4-409355d87599" :question/math-academy-id "q-117441" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{3}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-1,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038781/assets/0d7fcf48509464cdd6c22962b11c3db4fe24a08fa285adeeb9e30114c9667f49.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-1,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-([\\frac{1}{4}-1]-[\\frac{1}{4}+1]) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,3],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{81}{4}-3]-[\\frac{1}{4}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{69}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=2+18=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d63560-a0d8-5415-9e2b-e06b2e6b255b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299946} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0f2db7-aee0-5819-803a-19304673c8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299947} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb21fa3-63cd-5aa5-b814-04ddd2e08bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299948} {:answer/id #uuid "ed55fd76-a515-593b-8912-26836587633c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299949} {:answer/id #uuid "bf05e7d2-125e-5ccb-8398-911827524379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{50}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b0f2db7-aee0-5819-803a-19304673c8a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299947} :answer-field/id #uuid "532f2c76-6d6b-5a0f-8057-c1bf841a9505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fae3ee85-9a2b-5a43-a88b-f9ec14f24360" :question/math-academy-id "q-117454" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{5}+{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{3}+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=-1$ are our real solutions. Since $-1\\in [-2,0]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038781/assets/dc57ac72bdc0d496dc9e106c87d9c5f60cd5d8ca11d150f061edda690082cc71.png)\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{6}]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-1}^{0}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}]+[0-\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{49}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{25}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299951 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dab02288-1eea-59a7-b8a9-239d058a2b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299953} {:answer/id #uuid "ce064ce6-7d3b-5d82-84d4-e1c07dfb1d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce064ce6-7d3b-5d82-84d4-e1c07dfb1d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299954} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa50374-5532-5a6c-8c60-e502250b9239" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28206f20-bd4b-5f67-a0fa-9cc661c6940f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345643" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038781/assets/6344388d587e3ee32acf6efcd0d20f501f005cdc2c9d7b245391a8158fb0d23f.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\in [-1,2],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[{0}^{5}-(-1)^{5}]-\\frac{1}{2}[{0}^{4}-(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[0-(-1)]-\\frac{1}{2}[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,2],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[{2}^{5}-{0}^{5}]+\\frac{1}{2}[{2}^{4}-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[32]+\\frac{1}{2}[16] \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}+8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}+\\frac{8}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "262a1469-0920-565f-9268-28f0e5f1faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded Between the X-Axis and a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae083903-1ced-59b2-8087-be411f224b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{259}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae083903-1ced-59b2-8087-be411f224b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{259}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299957} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c2ddc2-743a-54d8-adf2-fefec3a0a0ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "28165b4c-9d72-5ff8-8d34-c314cd4cc3ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-361718" :question/problem "Find the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{4}-16$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{{x}^{4}} & =\\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [0,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038781/assets/bf221ae10e68a9c17efadd82b39264807b4dc00774fae7e30d9c0c9907368698.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-16)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative.\n\nTherefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+16{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}+16x\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[{2}^{5}-{0}^{5}]+16[2-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(32-0)+16(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}+32 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{5}+\\frac{160}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [2,3],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}({x}^{4}-16)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x-16{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}-16x\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[{3}^{5}-{2}^{5}]-16[3-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(243-32)-16(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{211}{5}-16 \\\\  & =\\frac{211}{5}-\\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{131}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{5}+\\frac{131}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d89ba41-6d24-548a-bd97-66830b752ba9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Area Bounded By a Curve When It Crosses the X-Axis Several Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0e5fc14-45c0-58f3-b0be-2a8cc4bb7d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299960} {:answer/id #uuid "161f44db-316a-5611-a7c1-16c22f55b129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{3}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299961} {:answer/id #uuid "16f3b1f8-3fa3-57ea-b483-02a87f8b9542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299962} {:answer/id #uuid "b4155446-5624-5a54-b27c-c6b6901ae306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}-(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299963} {:answer/id #uuid "f978be09-bd86-5c5f-85ac-120193015687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16f3b1f8-3fa3-57ea-b483-02a87f8b9542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299962} :answer-field/id #uuid "0955d86b-3e76-5af2-9044-339633727990" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90c85eb5-21b9-56cb-af99-71865688acba" :question/math-academy-id "q-54524" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038781/assets/c1906d5ad1ab55a028bcdb163e3066788dea44d928a53fda493ffe8972d369b6.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ -x({x}^{2}-x-6) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-2$, $x=0$ or $x=3.$ \nThis gives us two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-2,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,3]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{3}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6x+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}]}
